RECORDED AS BRAINSTORMING, NOT AS A PROPOSAL.

@ajs asked on 2026-10-22: "could we attempt to 'prove' the Riemann Hypothesis?", citing https://x.com/OxUniMaths/status/2112175656381419739. The post was NOT read, because X refused the fetch (HTTP 402). Then, on the observer's recommendation: "Record the Riemann map as an idea next in the proof ladder." Its PLACE on the ladder is his word: the rung after the heat equation. WHAT the rung is stays an idea.

THE PUSHBACK, RECORDED BESIDE IT, unchanged from the ladder's 10-18 note:
- Nobody here can prove it, and a design of an attempt has no checkable success.
- reflow2 records and checks the form of reasoning. It does not supply the missing mathematical idea.
- A "proof" written here would at best be a chain with a hidden gap. Exposing that kind of gap is exactly what reflow2 is built for.

THE MAP, A SKETCH TO TEST. Every citation below is AS RECALLED and UNCHECKED. Each must be checked against its source before the rung relies on it.
- THE CONJECTURE as the central claim, statement_kind conjecture. It must keep reading open, never shown.
- EQUIVALENT FORMS. Each equivalence is a proven theorem, cited; proving any one of these forms would prove RH:
  - Robin's inequality (Robin 1984): σ(n) < e^γ n ln ln n for all n > 5040;
  - Lagarias's elementary form (2002);
  - Li's criterion (1997);
  - the Nyman–Beurling criterion;
  - von Koch's error term for the prime-counting function (1901). Note: "RH gives the best possible error term", not "RH perfectly describes the primes".
- CONDITIONAL RESULTS that assume RH, each reading CONDITIONAL. A what-if that refutes RH should show exactly these fall, and the equivalences stand.
- PARTIAL RESULTS proven unconditionally:
  - zero-free regions;
  - the proportion of zeros proven to lie on the critical line: Levinson (1974) about 1/3; Conrey (1989) about 2/5.
  - A prediction-market page (octagonai.co) claims that in August 2026 a research version of Claude raised this proportion to about 67%. It is NOT confirmed against a primary source, so it is cited only if a primary source is found.
- COMPUTED EVIDENCE:
  - zeros verified numerically to a large height (the ladder's unchecked "10 trillion");
  - a flo2-calc check of Robin's inequality for 5040 < n ≤ N, which PROVES the bounded claim and is only EVIDENCE for RH. Does calc have a divisor-sum operator? Unchecked.

WHAT IT WOULD TEST, and why it is the next rung rather than a stunt:
- whether an open statement holds its reading under a large weight of evidence;
- whether conditional results spread correctly from one conjecture across many claims;
- whether equivalences (two-way inferences) read right, and propagate_from on RH shows conditional results falling while the equivalence theorems stand;
- whether cited analysis that cannot be worked through here is handled honestly as cited foundations.
Brocard–Ramanujan (rung 5) tested the same reading at small scale. This is the large-scale version.

SIZE (a finding): moderate. It is mostly cited theorems and their dependencies, plus one calc search. The citation checking is most of the work.

WHEN: after the heat-equation rung, which @ajs set running on 2026-10-22 ("Go with your recommendation for the heat equation"). Navier–Stokes stays the far end, and the same "map, not attempt" shape may suit it.

